PATCH 157: the trig library's SS=DS DGROUP base-loss (FUN_0000_05dd/05e2/0640/067a/0700/077e + the
recursion counter DAT_2000_dad0).

THE FINDING (asm-verified vs re_out/fist_dat_image.bin, definitive): this game runs SS=DS=DGROUP=0x1c00
(classic small model).  Ghidra's PrepAnalysis set a WRONG SS context (0x2ba9), so every `ss:`-override
DGROUP access was mislabelled to a bogus 0x2ba90-base DAT_2000_* address.  The trig library reads its
sin/cos/atan lookup tables via `ss:[bx+0xNNNN]` -> Ghidra dropped the segment base entirely, emitting
`*(int*)(iVar6+0x2042)` (a wild deref of low memory -> SIGSEGV) with 32-bit width (asm is a WORD load).

PROOF the base is 0x1c000 (DS), not 0x2ba90:
  * the sin table at DGROUP:0x2042 (linear 0x1e042) reads entry[64]=0x61f8=sin(22.5)*0xffff,
    entry[128]=0xb505=sin(45)*0xffff -- a valid quarter sin table.  At 0x2ba90+0x2042=0x2dad2 it is ASCII.
  * the recursion counter: FUN_0000_0665 does `inc BYTE ds:0x2040` (entry) / `dec BYTE ss:0x2040` (exit)
    -- ONE balanced counter at DGROUP:0x2040 (=0x1e040).  Ghidra labelled the DS inc as DAT_1000_e040
    (0x1e040, correct) and the SS-override dec as DAT_2000_dad0 (0x2dad0, wrong).  Same variable.
  * (companion, see revised patch 156) the LFSR RNG state at ss:0x1f84.. is written by the seeder
    (line ~46466: DAT_1000_df84 = 4b5e(...)) at DGROUP 0x1df84, and the RNG output ss:0x342 is read by
    the consumer DAT_1000_c342 (0x1c342, line ~44021) -- both DS-based.

FIX (all sites, asm-verified, faithful):
  * DAT_2000_dad0 #define rebased 0x2dad0 -> 0x1e040 (aliases DAT_1000_e040 -> the inc/dec/test now hit
    the one real counter; starts 0 in the image => the interpolation branch is taken as in the original).
  * every trig table read rebased to g_mem+0x1c000+off and narrowed to 16-bit (int16_t/uint16_t) to match
    the WORD load (sin 0x2042/0x2044; hi-res 0x2244/0x2246/0x2248; atan 0x2448/0x244a).

Reached ONLY by the trig library (mission-cascade math + voxel), not by any of the 19 verify flows (a bare
low-memory deref would have SIGSEGV'd them already) -> behaviour-neutral for verify; unblocks the ACCEPT
cascade past 9a32->03a9->05e2/05dd.

